4 Steps to Extend Your Data Architecture
Model Events, Not Objects
Stop designing tables around nouns. Design your system around verbs. Instead of an Account table that overwrites data, record every individual Transaction event. The current state is simply the sum of all past processes.
Prioritize Spatiotemporal Relations
Objects change over time. Your ontology must capture time natively. Use Temporal Ontologies (like W3C Time) to track how an entity transforms, splits, or merges across different time frames.
Use Graph-Based Interconnectedness
Process philosophy states that things are defined by their relations. Use a knowledge graph where edges (relationships) are first-class citizens and can hold data, change dynamically, and influence the nodes they connect.
Implement Functional Immutability
In process systems, the past cannot be changed. Implement append-only ledgers. If an error occurs, you do not modify the past record; you apply a new corrective process event.
